1
00:01:22,082 --> 00:01:24,779
Well, two states
down, two to go.

2
00:01:24,952 --> 00:01:26,921
Ahem. What's the
capital of Tennessee?

3
00:01:27,087 --> 00:01:28,180
- Memphis.
- Nope.

4
00:01:28,355 --> 00:01:30,688
- Yes, it is.
- Nashville.

5
00:01:30,857 --> 00:01:32,621
- Right.
- He looked at the map.

6
00:01:32,793 --> 00:01:33,817
Did not.

7
00:01:33,994 --> 00:01:35,792
Kevin, you've got the
map there on your lap.

8
00:01:35,963 --> 00:01:39,422
- Yeah, but I didn't look at it.
- Okay.

9
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,296
What is the capital
of South Carolina?

10
00:01:41,468 --> 00:01:43,300
- Raleigh.
- Nope.

11
00:01:43,470 --> 00:01:44,631
It is so.

12
00:01:44,805 --> 00:01:46,637
That is the capital
of North Carolina.

13
00:01:46,807 --> 00:01:49,709
- Yeah, dummy.
- Don't call me dummy, barf brain.

14
00:01:49,876 --> 00:01:52,141
- Stop it.
- Get him, Misty. Kill.

15
00:01:52,312 --> 00:01:54,747
- Please?
- Mom, she scratched me.

16
00:01:54,915 --> 00:01:56,795
Stop it. That cat is not
to be used as a weapon.

17
00:01:58,185 --> 00:01:59,517
What was that?

18
00:01:59,686 --> 00:02:02,155
Uh, I think we just blew a tire.

19
00:02:16,336 --> 00:02:18,032
Oh, for the love of Pete.

20
00:02:18,205 --> 00:02:19,468
- Charlie.
- Yeah, what?

21
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:20,972
Get over here.

22
00:02:21,141 --> 00:02:24,168
Look at this. This thing is so
crooked I get dizzy just looking at it.

23
00:02:24,344 --> 00:02:26,006
- What the hell are you thinking?
- Rusty.

24
00:02:26,613 --> 00:02:28,206
Ease up.

25
00:02:28,382 --> 00:02:30,582
If you think I'm gonna stand
by, watch this bum get paid

26
00:02:30,717 --> 00:02:32,845
the same as me for doing
lousy work, you're nuts.

27
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:35,080
You've made your point.
I'll take care of it, okay?

28
00:02:35,255 --> 00:02:37,019
- What'd you hire him for, anyway?
- Hey!

29
00:02:37,791 --> 00:02:39,487
That's enough.

30
00:02:40,827 --> 00:02:42,728
Go get back to work.

31
00:02:44,064 --> 00:02:46,064
Jackass doesn't even
know how to lay a brick, Jack.

32
00:02:46,233 --> 00:02:48,327
We'll talk more about
this tonight, Rusty.

33
00:02:50,137 --> 00:02:52,538
Now, why don't you
get back to work?

34
00:03:01,348 --> 00:03:03,214
There you go.

35
00:03:12,159 --> 00:03:15,823
Okay, this is it. Let's get out.

36
00:03:16,730 --> 00:03:18,426
We gotta stay here?

37
00:03:18,598 --> 00:03:21,198
- Yeah, we gotta stay here.
- You said there'd be a swimming pool.

38
00:03:21,368 --> 00:03:23,530
Well, I thought we'd get to
Chattanooga, but we didn't.

39
00:03:23,704 --> 00:03:25,866
- Oh, Mom.
- Now, come on, get your backpacks.

40
00:03:30,243 --> 00:03:31,472
Oh, Molly, you let Misty out.

41
00:03:31,645 --> 00:03:33,045
Misty!

42
00:03:33,213 --> 00:03:34,408
Go get that cat.

43
00:03:34,581 --> 00:03:36,277
Here, Misty!

44
00:03:36,450 --> 00:03:39,545
- Here, kitty, kitty, kitty.
- Here, Misty.

45
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:41,120
Damn.

46
00:03:45,258 --> 00:03:48,092
You think I'm nothing,
don't you, Jack?

47
00:03:48,395 --> 00:03:51,395
You think you can pull this kind of crap
because I'm just some dime-a-dozen,

48
00:03:51,531 --> 00:03:54,729
cement-mucking grunt,
don't you, Jack? Don't you?

49
00:03:54,901 --> 00:03:56,426
Get out of here.

50
00:04:01,908 --> 00:04:04,173
You're gonna pay for this, Jack.

51
00:04:04,578 --> 00:04:06,274
Big time.

52
00:04:13,353 --> 00:04:15,481
- You can go to hell too.
- Unh!

53
00:04:17,324 --> 00:04:18,952
Dad.

54
00:04:19,893 --> 00:04:22,590
Dad, are you okay?

55
00:04:22,763 --> 00:04:24,459
- Are you all right?
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

56
00:04:24,631 --> 00:04:26,691
I'm fine. I'm fine.

57
00:04:27,667 --> 00:04:30,660
- What was that all about?
- I fired him. He didn't take it too well.

58
00:04:30,837 --> 00:04:32,430
God, no kidding.

59
00:04:32,606 --> 00:04:34,370
I came by to take
you to dinner. Jeez.

60
00:04:34,808 --> 00:04:37,209
Looks like I should take
you to the emergency room.

61
00:04:37,377 --> 00:04:38,936
No.

62
00:04:39,112 --> 00:04:40,341
No need.

63
00:04:40,514 --> 00:04:42,915
Dinner will be just fine.

64
00:04:49,322 --> 00:04:51,362
<i>Everybody had a chance
to get a fresh cup of coffee.</i>

65
00:04:51,525 --> 00:04:52,725
<i>We're back here at</i> Phone Talk.

66
00:04:52,893 --> 00:04:55,333
<i>And we're lucky enough to have
with us this morning the author</i>

67
00:04:55,462 --> 00:04:58,921
<i>of last year's hilarious...</i>

68
00:05:41,241 --> 00:05:42,961
All right, now we are
all gonna go in there.

69
00:05:43,109 --> 00:05:44,600
Kevin, I want you
to close your eyes.

70
00:05:44,778 --> 00:05:48,442
Keep them covered until
I say so, okay? Let's go.

71
00:05:49,416 --> 00:05:52,352
All right. Go on. Go straight.

72
00:06:01,194 --> 00:06:02,719
Hold on. Come here.

73
00:06:02,896 --> 00:06:04,694
Okay, Kevin.

74
00:06:05,532 --> 00:06:07,091
Wow, Mom.

75
00:06:07,267 --> 00:06:10,032
- What?
- You look great.

76
00:06:10,203 --> 00:06:12,866
Thanks, Kevin. I
needed to hear that.

77
00:06:13,039 --> 00:06:15,304
Okay, onward and upward.

78
00:06:16,810 --> 00:06:18,711
- Slow down.
- Oh, yes, yes.

79
00:06:18,879 --> 00:06:20,609
All right. Here we go.

80
00:06:26,686 --> 00:06:28,746
Excuse me. My
name is Katie Clark.

81
00:06:28,922 --> 00:06:31,153
- The district attorney is expecting me.
- Indeed, I am.

82
00:06:31,324 --> 00:06:33,418
- Richard Jenkins.
- Katie Clark.

83
00:06:33,593 --> 00:06:34,788
Everybody, listen up.

84
00:06:35,562 --> 00:06:38,896
This is Katie Clark,
our newest assistant DA

85
00:06:41,334 --> 00:06:42,393
I have my children with me

86
00:06:42,569 --> 00:06:44,595
because we drove here
straight from St. Louis.

87
00:06:44,771 --> 00:06:47,764
Normally, they'd be in school
right now. Public school.

88
00:06:47,941 --> 00:06:50,103
I'm a firm believer in
the public school system.

89
00:06:50,810 --> 00:06:52,972
Do they have names?

90
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:55,481
Of course, they do.

91
00:06:55,649 --> 00:06:57,845
This is Molly and this is Kevin.

92
00:06:59,019 --> 00:07:01,113
Police just brought
in a murder suspect.

93
00:07:01,288 --> 00:07:04,258
Interview Room 2, around the
corner, second door on your left.

94
00:07:04,424 --> 00:07:06,484
Welcome aboard.

95
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:15,769
- Mister...?
- Stonehill. Steven Stonehill.

96
00:07:15,936 --> 00:07:18,167
Mr. Jenkins wants me
to go question a suspect.

97
00:07:18,338 --> 00:07:19,601
Since I don't have an office,

98
00:07:19,773 --> 00:07:22,013
I was wondering if I could
leave a few of my things here.

99
00:07:22,175 --> 00:07:24,144
- Yeah, sure, be my guest.
- Great.

100
00:07:28,982 --> 00:07:30,348
Thanks.

101
00:07:40,393 --> 00:07:42,521
Oh, now, wait a minute.

102
00:07:56,910 --> 00:07:59,209
- Katie?
- Jack.

103
00:07:59,379 --> 00:08:01,575
I had a terrible feeling
this might be you.

104
00:08:01,748 --> 00:08:03,307
What are you doing here?

105
00:08:03,483 --> 00:08:06,146
I thought you moved to
Cincinnati or something.

106
00:08:06,319 --> 00:08:09,153
St. Louis. Just moved back.

107
00:08:09,322 --> 00:08:11,848
I'm the new assistant DA.

108
00:08:12,459 --> 00:08:13,984
How did you get
charged with murder?

109
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:15,219
Katie, it is a mistake.

110
00:08:15,395 --> 00:08:18,593
I'll say. My first day, my
first five minutes on the job,

111
00:08:18,765 --> 00:08:20,757
and whose case do
they assign me to?

112
00:08:20,934 --> 00:08:22,266
I don't believe it.

113
00:08:22,635 --> 00:08:23,830
Well, maybe it's fate.

114
00:08:24,004 --> 00:08:26,906
It is a conflict of interest, Jack.
So don't say one more word.

115
00:08:27,073 --> 00:08:29,372
- Let me get the DA to send someone.
- Katie, Katie.

116
00:08:29,976 --> 00:08:31,604
Now, come on.

117
00:08:31,778 --> 00:08:33,974
You of all people know
that I couldn't have done

118
00:08:34,147 --> 00:08:35,843
what they're accusing me of.

119
00:08:36,016 --> 00:08:37,814
Look, save it for your lawyer.

120
00:08:37,984 --> 00:08:40,283
And from what I've seen
just glancing through this file,

121
00:08:40,453 --> 00:08:41,887
he or she better be a good one.

122
00:08:42,422 --> 00:08:45,415
Katie, come on.

123
00:08:45,959 --> 00:08:48,622
I mean, at least this way, we
could have dinner sometime.

124
00:08:50,196 --> 00:08:53,030
We did all that four
years ago, Jack.

125
00:08:53,199 --> 00:08:54,792
Just let it go, okay?

126
00:09:08,181 --> 00:09:10,301
Excuse me. I need to talk
to Mr. Jenkins. It's urgent.

127
00:09:10,450 --> 00:09:11,975
I'm sorry. He's on
a conference call.

128
00:09:12,152 --> 00:09:13,984
Right after that, he's
meeting with the mayor.

129
00:09:14,154 --> 00:09:18,114
- Ahem. Lest we forget.
- Thanks.

130
00:09:18,291 --> 00:09:19,452
How'd it go? Heh.

131
00:09:19,626 --> 00:09:22,095
Well, he cheats at every
game you try to play with him.

132
00:09:22,262 --> 00:09:23,286
Do not.

133
00:09:23,463 --> 00:09:26,126
And she is absolutely
100 percent positive

134
00:09:26,299 --> 00:09:28,928
- that the capital of Montana is Billings.
- Well, it is.

135
00:09:29,102 --> 00:09:31,182
But other than that, it
was a little slice of heaven.

136
00:09:31,337 --> 00:09:34,501
- I did not cheat.
- Let's eat.

137
00:09:34,674 --> 00:09:37,473
Come on, let the
people do their work.

138
00:09:38,478 --> 00:09:39,844
Come on.

139
00:09:40,013 --> 00:09:43,142
- I want a hamburger. Hamburger.
- Pizza. Pizza.

140
00:09:43,316 --> 00:09:45,285
Hey, wait a minute, wait
a minute, wait a minute.

141
00:09:45,452 --> 00:09:47,683
This has been a tough day,
we haven't been to the house.

142
00:09:47,854 --> 00:09:50,085
So here are the rules. I
am the only grown-up here,

143
00:09:50,256 --> 00:09:52,521
so I not only get to
make decisions, I have to.

144
00:09:52,692 --> 00:09:54,661
- Got it?
- Yes, ma'am.

145
00:09:54,828 --> 00:09:56,296
- Got it?
- Okay.

146
00:09:56,463 --> 00:09:58,523
- Okay?
- Yes, ma'am.

147
00:09:58,698 --> 00:09:59,722
Thank you.

148
00:09:59,899 --> 00:10:01,299
Now, we're gonna
get in that car,

149
00:10:01,468 --> 00:10:03,348
and the first place we
come to that serves food,

150
00:10:03,503 --> 00:10:05,768
whatever it is, that's
what we're gonna eat.

151
00:10:05,939 --> 00:10:07,965
- Now, come on.
- Hope it's hamburger.

152
00:10:08,141 --> 00:10:09,803
- Pizza.
- Whatever they serve.

153
00:10:18,184 --> 00:10:21,780
Forensics found,
uh, traces of your skin

154
00:10:21,955 --> 00:10:24,789
under two of Russ
Stambler's fingernails.

155
00:10:24,958 --> 00:10:27,052
Yeah, we got in a fight
last night at the house.

156
00:10:27,794 --> 00:10:28,989
That's when he scratched me.

157
00:10:29,162 --> 00:10:32,496
I was there. Rusty
was out of control.

158
00:10:32,665 --> 00:10:34,827
- How come?
- I fired him.

159
00:10:35,068 --> 00:10:37,003
Oh. Tsk.

160
00:10:37,337 --> 00:10:39,533
Well, let us see...

161
00:10:40,773 --> 00:10:44,904
Oh, yeah, where were you
between 7 and 8 this morning?

162
00:10:45,078 --> 00:10:47,172
I was stuck in traffic
on my way to a job,

163
00:10:47,347 --> 00:10:48,645
just like I am every morning.

164
00:10:49,149 --> 00:10:52,313
Yeah. It says that your
fingerprints were all over

165
00:10:52,485 --> 00:10:56,445
Rusty's front door
and his living room.

166
00:10:57,824 --> 00:10:59,224
Yes, Mr. Matlock.

167
00:10:59,392 --> 00:11:02,157
You see, uh, Rusty
worked for me.

168
00:11:02,328 --> 00:11:05,093
So he had been to my house a
couple of times. I'd been to his.

169
00:11:05,265 --> 00:11:06,790
- You were friends?
- Yeah.

170
00:11:06,966 --> 00:11:08,867
We'd had a couple of
drinks over the years.

171
00:11:09,369 --> 00:11:10,496
Why'd you fire him?

172
00:11:10,670 --> 00:11:13,435
Because he was causing
problems on the job.

173
00:11:13,606 --> 00:11:18,010
Now, look, Mr. Matlock, no disrespect,
but I wanted Mike to handle my case.

174
00:11:19,145 --> 00:11:22,115
Oh, well, why didn't you?

175
00:11:22,282 --> 00:11:23,306
I do corporate work.

176
00:11:23,483 --> 00:11:26,248
Dad, you've got to
have a criminal lawyer.

177
00:11:26,419 --> 00:11:27,739
It's not a good
idea for a client

178
00:11:27,887 --> 00:11:29,856
to be personally
involved with his attorney.

179
00:11:30,023 --> 00:11:31,184
That's right.

180
00:11:31,724 --> 00:11:35,354
And, in a small
way, I am with you.

181
00:11:35,562 --> 00:11:39,624
Remember, you were supposed to
do some work on my house last year.

182
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:42,096
Well, I gave you a bid, yeah,

183
00:11:42,268 --> 00:11:43,566
but I never heard from you.

184
00:11:43,736 --> 00:11:46,535
No, that's because your
bid was highway robbery.

185
00:11:46,706 --> 00:11:49,540
- My bid was very reasonable.
- Reasonable.

186
00:11:49,709 --> 00:11:52,975
I'd have wound up paying you
more than I paid for the whole house.

187
00:11:53,146 --> 00:11:56,480
Mr. Matlock, you bought
this house 40 years ago.

188
00:11:56,649 --> 00:11:58,675
Wait a minute.
Mr. Matlock, how about this?

189
00:11:58,885 --> 00:12:02,879
A check for your fee,
in full, and up front.

190
00:12:11,030 --> 00:12:12,589
Hm.

191
00:12:14,667 --> 00:12:19,432
Uh, and if I, oh, say,

192
00:12:20,340 --> 00:12:25,278
wanted to have new hardwood
floors put in the house...?

193
00:12:28,581 --> 00:12:29,947
Tsk.

194
00:12:31,517 --> 00:12:33,850
I will see what I can do.

195
00:12:34,921 --> 00:12:36,389
Okay.

196
00:12:49,168 --> 00:12:50,534
Mr. Jenkins?

197
00:12:50,703 --> 00:12:53,002
Well, good morning. Come in.

198
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:57,274
How's that case I gave you,
the Gibson case? How's it going?

199
00:12:57,443 --> 00:12:59,243
Well, that's what I
need to talk to you about.

200
00:12:59,412 --> 00:13:01,212
Sorry I had to dump
something like that on you

201
00:13:01,381 --> 00:13:02,974
not two minutes
after you walked in,

202
00:13:03,149 --> 00:13:05,983
but as you probably
noticed, we're swamped.

203
00:13:06,152 --> 00:13:09,054
One of the partners in that
firm you were with in St. Louis

204
00:13:09,222 --> 00:13:11,088
did say you were an
incredibly quick study.

205
00:13:11,257 --> 00:13:13,886
- Well, I am, but...
- And truth be told,

206
00:13:14,060 --> 00:13:15,688
he raved about you.

207
00:13:15,862 --> 00:13:18,559
He said you were the most
dedicated, hardest-working associate

208
00:13:18,731 --> 00:13:20,529
they had ever hired.

209
00:13:21,067 --> 00:13:22,091
He seemed to think

210
00:13:22,268 --> 00:13:25,705
it was because you were raising two
children on your own, not in spite of it.

211
00:13:25,872 --> 00:13:28,865
- Ha, I tend to overcompensate, but...
- And when I heard that,

212
00:13:29,042 --> 00:13:31,375
I said to myself, "Bingo."

213
00:13:31,544 --> 00:13:33,911
Don't get me wrong.
I'm not a chauvinist

214
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:35,241
or in any way against women,

215
00:13:35,415 --> 00:13:38,249
but you wouldn't believe how
many times I have been burned

216
00:13:38,418 --> 00:13:41,616
by women who consider
working here not a job

217
00:13:41,788 --> 00:13:43,450
but as something they
kind of squeezed in

218
00:13:43,623 --> 00:13:45,922
between personal crises.

219
00:13:46,726 --> 00:13:49,753
Medical, marital,
sexual, spiritual.

220
00:13:50,863 --> 00:13:52,627
Anyway, I am glad you are here.

221
00:13:52,799 --> 00:13:54,461
You are a breath of fresh air.

222
00:13:56,569 --> 00:13:59,095
- So the Gibson case?
- Yes?

223
00:13:59,505 --> 00:14:01,940
You said earlier you needed
to talk to me about something.

224
00:14:03,910 --> 00:14:06,106
Well, I just wanted to tell you

225
00:14:06,279 --> 00:14:10,842
that we will probably be filing charges
against him by the end of the day.

226
00:14:12,785 --> 00:14:14,014
Go to it. Good luck.

227
00:14:14,654 --> 00:14:16,088
Thanks.

228
00:14:23,529 --> 00:14:26,590
So in whose custody are
the little darlings today?

229
00:14:27,300 --> 00:14:28,962
The Board of Education's.

230
00:14:29,135 --> 00:14:30,775
I'm sorry for what I
did to you yesterday.

231
00:14:30,903 --> 00:14:33,372
I was just... I'm sorry.

232
00:14:34,073 --> 00:14:36,565
Well, word is Gibson's
hired himself a lawyer.

233
00:14:36,743 --> 00:14:38,644
Ben Matlock.

234
00:14:39,045 --> 00:14:40,843
Welcome to the big leagues.

235
00:14:47,353 --> 00:14:51,757
I'd like to get the old place up
to snuff, you know, within reason.

236
00:14:51,924 --> 00:14:57,522
Uh, how much would it cost
to put central air in the house?

237
00:14:57,697 --> 00:15:00,428
Mr. Matlock, off the top of
my head, I just don't know.

238
00:15:00,600 --> 00:15:03,126
So I'll tell you what, why
don't I get back to you, okay?

239
00:15:03,302 --> 00:15:06,204
Yeah. And the heating
system could use some...

240
00:15:06,372 --> 00:15:10,036
- Watch out, that step's broken.
- Uh, oh, yeah. Huh. Ha, ha, ha.

241
00:15:10,209 --> 00:15:12,542
Just like my steps. Ha, ha.

242
00:15:12,812 --> 00:15:16,374
Whole front porch is just
kind of starting to list a little bit.

243
00:15:16,549 --> 00:15:19,212
Could you get back to me
on that? How are you doing?

244
00:15:30,163 --> 00:15:33,099
- So this is where it happened?
- Yep.

245
00:15:33,599 --> 00:15:36,125
- Rusty married?
- No.

246
00:15:36,302 --> 00:15:37,827
Divorced?

247
00:15:38,004 --> 00:15:39,870
- I don't think so.
- Hm.

248
00:15:45,778 --> 00:15:47,406
Hm.

249
00:15:48,581 --> 00:15:50,447
Well.

250
00:15:51,684 --> 00:15:54,085
Well, he had a girlfriend.

251
00:15:55,188 --> 00:15:57,350
Yeah, I guess he did.

252
00:17:11,097 --> 00:17:13,089
- Come on, Mom.
- Kevin.

253
00:17:13,266 --> 00:17:15,098
I like to paint.

254
00:17:15,268 --> 00:17:17,828
Let me do it, please?

255
00:17:18,004 --> 00:17:20,997
No, honey, you're too messy.
You'd get it all over the place.

256
00:17:21,173 --> 00:17:24,337
- I'll be careful. Come on, Mom.
- Mom?

257
00:17:24,510 --> 00:17:25,671
Just a minute, Molly.

258
00:17:25,845 --> 00:17:28,713
Please, please, please?

259
00:17:28,881 --> 00:17:31,646
Okay, okay, look, you can
paint Grandpa's rocking chair

260
00:17:31,817 --> 00:17:34,753
down in the basement,
okay? As soon as I'm finished.

261
00:17:35,054 --> 00:17:38,183
- Yay! Thanks, Mom.
- Ha, ha, ha!

262
00:17:40,159 --> 00:17:41,752
Mom?

263
00:17:42,862 --> 00:17:44,353
This guy here
wants to talk to you.

264
00:17:44,530 --> 00:17:47,022
- Hi, I'm Ben Matlock.
- Hi. Uh...

265
00:17:47,199 --> 00:17:49,430
- Katie Clark.
- Hi, Katie.

266
00:17:49,602 --> 00:17:52,071
Mom, how come Kevin gets
to paint something and I don't?

267
00:17:52,238 --> 00:17:54,901
- Honey, we'll talk about it later, okay?
- I get to help.

268
00:17:56,776 --> 00:17:59,177
- Yeah. Come in, please. Sit down.
- Thank you.

269
00:17:59,345 --> 00:18:03,908
- Can I get you something?
- Oh, no, thank you. I'm fine. Ahem.

270
00:18:05,351 --> 00:18:08,844
- Did Jack tell you I questioned him?
- Yes.

271
00:18:09,956 --> 00:18:12,323
It's a shame you
don't know Jack.

272
00:18:12,491 --> 00:18:15,017
If you did, you'd
know he's not a killer.

273
00:18:17,296 --> 00:18:19,492
That's what all
defense lawyers say.

274
00:18:19,665 --> 00:18:21,725
I, ha, ha, I guess that's right.

275
00:18:22,535 --> 00:18:23,969
So...

276
00:18:24,136 --> 00:18:30,167
Oh, I just wanted to introduce
myself and chat a little about the case.

277
00:18:30,710 --> 00:18:31,973
Oh, good.

278
00:18:32,144 --> 00:18:35,342
Well, I think things are
starting to shape up quite nicely.

279
00:18:35,514 --> 00:18:38,746
Yeah. But you
don't have a motive.

280
00:18:39,619 --> 00:18:42,589
Uh, no, I guess I don't.

281
00:18:42,755 --> 00:18:45,054
But, you know, I can't
help think how odd it was

282
00:18:45,224 --> 00:18:47,784
that your client invited Rusty
Stambler over to his house

283
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:50,657
the night before the murder
just to fire him. Why his house?

284
00:18:50,830 --> 00:18:52,264
Why not fire him on the job?

285
00:18:52,431 --> 00:18:54,798
Oh, well, Jack's a nice man,

286
00:18:54,967 --> 00:18:58,460
and he was just trying to
soften the blow, that's all.

287
00:18:59,538 --> 00:19:01,734
You know what I
find odd, though?

288
00:19:01,907 --> 00:19:04,502
- What's that?
- Rusty's girlfriend.

289
00:19:04,677 --> 00:19:07,272
She was there the
night before the murder.

290
00:19:07,446 --> 00:19:11,042
She left her makeup
kit and never came back.

291
00:19:12,018 --> 00:19:13,577
- Wonder why.
- I don't know.

292
00:19:14,086 --> 00:19:15,918
Could be important.

293
00:19:16,689 --> 00:19:18,783
- Yeah.
- I wouldn't have thought much about it

294
00:19:18,958 --> 00:19:20,483
if I hadn't seen the kit.

295
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:24,988
Sometimes seeing
things makes a difference.

296
00:19:25,164 --> 00:19:30,831
That's why I like to go over
the murder scene personally.

297
00:19:31,103 --> 00:19:33,800
- Mm. Me too.
- Yeah.

298
00:19:35,708 --> 00:19:38,177
- Gotta find out who left this here.
- We're working on it.

299
00:19:38,344 --> 00:19:40,540
- Put more men on it.
- We've already got the killer.

300
00:19:40,713 --> 00:19:43,410
We've made an arrest.
I've gotta get a conviction.

301
00:19:43,582 --> 00:19:45,210
If we don't find out
who left that here,

302
00:19:45,384 --> 00:19:49,048
Ben Matlock's gonna make a
case that maybe she was the killer.

303
00:19:49,855 --> 00:19:52,055
Don't suppose you've ID'd
the prints on that wine glass?

304
00:19:52,224 --> 00:19:54,989
Haven't heard back
from the lab, ma'am.

305
00:19:57,430 --> 00:19:59,296
Check out this number?

306
00:19:59,699 --> 00:20:02,259
We've been doing
this a long time.

307
00:20:03,069 --> 00:20:04,503
Yes, ma'am.

308
00:20:04,670 --> 00:20:07,765
The guy said he never
heard of Rusty Stambler.

309
00:20:07,940 --> 00:20:09,203
Really?

310
00:20:09,375 --> 00:20:11,276
That's odd.

311
00:20:27,460 --> 00:20:29,224
Hello, Katie.

312
00:20:32,098 --> 00:20:34,829
- How you been?
- Fine.

313
00:20:35,701 --> 00:20:37,397
I thought you were
quitting the case.

314
00:20:37,970 --> 00:20:41,532
Believe me, I wanted
to, but I couldn't.

315
00:20:42,808 --> 00:20:44,572
I understand that.

316
00:20:44,744 --> 00:20:46,178
No, I don't think you do.

317
00:20:46,846 --> 00:20:49,406
I'm gonna prosecute this case
to the very best of my ability.

318
00:20:49,582 --> 00:20:52,051
No punches pulled,
no holds barred.

319
00:20:52,218 --> 00:20:53,379
I wouldn't do that, Katie.

320
00:20:53,552 --> 00:20:56,078
- Why not?
- You're gonna look very foolish.

321
00:20:56,255 --> 00:20:57,518
I didn't do it.

322
00:21:03,095 --> 00:21:06,532
These fibers were removed
from the top of a chain-link fence

323
00:21:06,699 --> 00:21:08,691
that runs behind the
decedent's house.

324
00:21:08,868 --> 00:21:10,461
When were they removed?

325
00:21:10,636 --> 00:21:14,300
On the 12th of
October at 2:20 p.m.

326
00:21:14,473 --> 00:21:15,964
Approximately four
and a half hours

327
00:21:16,142 --> 00:21:18,338
after Mr. Stambler's
body was discovered.

328
00:21:18,511 --> 00:21:20,673
Do you have any idea
where these fibers came from?

329
00:21:21,113 --> 00:21:24,550
They came from a brand of
blue jeans known as Strong Man.

330
00:21:25,117 --> 00:21:27,018
This pair of jeans
was recently removed,

331
00:21:27,186 --> 00:21:29,621
by warrant, from the
defendant's bedroom closet.

332
00:21:29,789 --> 00:21:32,691
Would you tell the court
what brand they are, please?

333
00:21:35,294 --> 00:21:36,557
They're Strong Man.

334
00:21:36,729 --> 00:21:38,561
This tear in the left leg here,

335
00:21:38,731 --> 00:21:40,700
how, in your expert
opinion, was it made?

336
00:21:41,066 --> 00:21:45,094
Well, the edges are frayed,
which indicates fibers are missing,

337
00:21:45,271 --> 00:21:48,605
so I'd say it was the result
of a snag rather than a cut.

338
00:21:48,774 --> 00:21:51,471
Is it possible that the fibers
that were found on the fence

339
00:21:51,644 --> 00:21:54,375
behind the decedent's house
came from this pair of jeans?

340
00:21:54,547 --> 00:21:57,779
Given the length of the fibers
and the length of the tear, yes.

341
00:21:57,950 --> 00:21:59,543
I'd say it was very possible.

342
00:22:00,152 --> 00:22:01,279
Thank you.

343
00:22:01,453 --> 00:22:03,319
Nothing further.

344
00:22:06,492 --> 00:22:09,052
Um, Mr. Pratt,

345
00:22:09,728 --> 00:22:15,167
how many Americans would you
say own a pair of Strong Man jeans?

346
00:22:15,334 --> 00:22:17,428
Objection. Calls
for speculation.

347
00:22:18,137 --> 00:22:20,163
You're the one that
said he was an expert.

348
00:22:20,339 --> 00:22:21,898
Let the man talk.

349
00:22:22,074 --> 00:22:23,337
Objection overruled.

350
00:22:23,809 --> 00:22:25,209
Thank you.

351
00:22:25,377 --> 00:22:29,178
Would you say, uh, in the
thousands? The tens of thousands?

352
00:22:30,249 --> 00:22:32,013
Hundreds of thousands, at least.

353
00:22:32,218 --> 00:22:34,517
Ah. And of those
hundreds of thousands,

354
00:22:34,687 --> 00:22:37,987
how many would you say
have a tear in the pants leg?

355
00:22:39,692 --> 00:22:41,684
Well, seeing as how
it's considered stylish

356
00:22:41,861 --> 00:22:43,090
for some members of society

357
00:22:43,262 --> 00:22:46,130
to walk around with
gaping holes in their jeans,

358
00:22:46,298 --> 00:22:47,960
I would imagine quite a few.

359
00:22:48,400 --> 00:22:50,301
Heh, heh. I agree. I agree.

360
00:22:50,469 --> 00:22:51,698
Thank you. No more questions.

361
00:22:52,271 --> 00:22:53,830
The witness may step down.

362
00:22:56,942 --> 00:22:58,069
For its next witness,

363
00:22:58,244 --> 00:23:00,679
the state would like
to call Mr. Nick Murrow.

364
00:23:15,060 --> 00:23:16,187
Mr. Murrow,

365
00:23:16,362 --> 00:23:18,024
did you know the
decedent, Rusty Stambler?

366
00:23:18,497 --> 00:23:19,521
No.

367
00:23:19,698 --> 00:23:21,291
You never had any
contact with him?

368
00:23:21,467 --> 00:23:24,266
- No.
- What's your phone number?

369
00:23:24,436 --> 00:23:26,530
555-6893.

370
00:23:26,705 --> 00:23:29,641
If you never had any
contact with Rusty Stambler,

371
00:23:29,808 --> 00:23:31,470
then why was your
phone number found

372
00:23:31,644 --> 00:23:34,580
in his bedroom on a newspaper
delivered the morning of the murder?

373
00:23:35,247 --> 00:23:36,967
And why, according to
phone company records,

374
00:23:37,116 --> 00:23:38,812
did he call you on October 12th,

375
00:23:38,984 --> 00:23:41,078
the day he was
murdered at 6:45 a.m.?

376
00:23:42,288 --> 00:23:43,916
Must've had the wrong number.

377
00:23:44,089 --> 00:23:45,955
If he dialed your
number by mistake,

378
00:23:46,125 --> 00:23:49,584
why was the phone call two
minutes and 12 seconds long?

379
00:23:53,032 --> 00:23:55,558
I see you're wearing a
brace. What happened?

380
00:23:55,734 --> 00:23:58,863
- I threw my back out at work.
- How'd you do that?

381
00:23:59,038 --> 00:24:01,439
Well, I was working a
site up at Lyndon Hills,

382
00:24:01,607 --> 00:24:04,771
and I stepped on some cement that
was supposed to be set, only it wasn't.

383
00:24:04,944 --> 00:24:07,573
Uh, caved in and
my foot got caught.

384
00:24:07,746 --> 00:24:09,510
I fell over sideways
and sprained my back.

385
00:24:09,682 --> 00:24:12,049
- So you're a construction worker?
- A plumber.

386
00:24:12,217 --> 00:24:14,049
Have you been able to
work since the accident?

387
00:24:14,219 --> 00:24:15,915
Oh, no way. I can
barely get out of bed.

388
00:24:16,088 --> 00:24:17,750
So you filed an insurance claim?

389
00:24:18,891 --> 00:24:20,917
- No.
- Why not?

390
00:24:21,527 --> 00:24:23,120
Just seemed like a hassle.

391
00:24:24,797 --> 00:24:27,028
According to your bank records,

392
00:24:27,199 --> 00:24:29,964
about a week after the accident,

393
00:24:30,135 --> 00:24:35,472
you deposited close to $60,000
into your savings account.

394
00:24:35,641 --> 00:24:37,109
Where did that money come from?

395
00:24:41,080 --> 00:24:42,571
It came from that
general contractor

396
00:24:42,748 --> 00:24:45,343
for that house out in
Lyndon Hills, didn't it?

397
00:24:45,517 --> 00:24:48,385
Isn't it true he paid you off to keep
you from filing an insurance claim

398
00:24:48,554 --> 00:24:50,113
because he didn't
want anybody asking

399
00:24:50,289 --> 00:24:51,552
why that cement gave way?

400
00:24:54,626 --> 00:24:57,095
Who am I talking about, Nick?

401
00:24:57,262 --> 00:24:59,561
Who was that general contractor?

402
00:25:00,566 --> 00:25:01,659
Jack Gibson.

403
00:25:01,834 --> 00:25:03,996
Jack Gibson, the defendant.

404
00:25:04,370 --> 00:25:07,397
- Tell us how that cement gave way.
- I don't know.

405
00:25:07,573 --> 00:25:08,905
No?

406
00:25:09,074 --> 00:25:11,407
Didn't Mr. Gibson tell you?

407
00:25:11,577 --> 00:25:14,206
Or what about Rusty
Stambler? Didn't he tell you?

408
00:25:14,380 --> 00:25:15,746
When he called you that morning?

409
00:25:15,914 --> 00:25:18,315
He warned you he was about to
blow the whistle on Jack Gibson

410
00:25:18,484 --> 00:25:20,316
for using undersized
and rusted out rebar

411
00:25:20,486 --> 00:25:22,079
to reinforce that cement pad.

412
00:25:22,254 --> 00:25:24,553
Rebar that was
four times as cheap

413
00:25:24,723 --> 00:25:27,488
as the steel Mr. Gibson indicated
he was going to use on his bid

414
00:25:27,659 --> 00:25:29,787
and for which he
billed his client.

415
00:25:29,962 --> 00:25:33,126
You injured your back because
Jack Gibson committed fraud.

416
00:25:33,298 --> 00:25:36,063
That's a lie! She's making
this up to get even with me

417
00:25:36,235 --> 00:25:37,328
for breaking up with her!

418
00:25:37,503 --> 00:25:39,597
- Like hell I am!
- Dad, sit down, Dad.

419
00:25:39,772 --> 00:25:41,604
That's why she's doing it!

420
00:25:41,774 --> 00:25:45,108
This court will come to order.
The defendant will sit down. Silence.

421
00:25:45,878 --> 00:25:47,471
That means everyone.

422
00:25:53,685 --> 00:25:55,085
Are you saying it's true?

423
00:25:55,254 --> 00:25:56,654
You had an intimate relationship

424
00:25:56,822 --> 00:25:58,688
with the man you
are now prosecuting?

425
00:25:58,857 --> 00:25:59,916
Yes.

426
00:26:00,092 --> 00:26:03,529
Miss Clark, you have
not exactly made my day.

427
00:26:03,695 --> 00:26:06,494
Not only am I going to
have to declare a mistrial,

428
00:26:06,665 --> 00:26:08,395
but because you spearheaded

429
00:26:08,567 --> 00:26:11,002
the initial investigation
against Mr. Gibson,

430
00:26:11,170 --> 00:26:14,868
you may very well have
compromised the state's whole case.

431
00:26:15,040 --> 00:26:16,668
How could you let this happen?

432
00:26:16,842 --> 00:26:18,071
I didn't know. I swear.

433
00:26:18,243 --> 00:26:20,474
I had a very rocky first
day here, Your Honor.

434
00:26:20,646 --> 00:26:21,978
If you'd just let me explain.

435
00:26:22,147 --> 00:26:25,345
- I've heard enough, Miss Clark.
- But you haven't heard the truth.

436
00:26:28,520 --> 00:26:30,580
Now, four years ago
when I was living here

437
00:26:30,756 --> 00:26:32,247
and doing legal work part-time,

438
00:26:32,424 --> 00:26:34,416
my husband took off
with another woman.

439
00:26:34,593 --> 00:26:37,825
Divorced me and left
me and our two children.

440
00:26:37,996 --> 00:26:41,865
Now, Jack Gibson, who up till then
had been one of my husband's friends,

441
00:26:42,034 --> 00:26:44,230
helped us out and
became my friend.

442
00:26:44,403 --> 00:26:47,430
And pretty soon, he
became more than a friend.

443
00:26:47,973 --> 00:26:51,375
He helped me get back on my feet,
kind of renewed my self-confidence.

444
00:26:51,543 --> 00:26:54,536
But by the time I accepted
a full-time job in St. Louis,

445
00:26:54,713 --> 00:26:56,579
we were back to
being just friends.

446
00:26:56,748 --> 00:26:58,944
And contrary to what Mr. Gibson
would have you believe,

447
00:26:59,118 --> 00:27:00,416
we parted on good terms.

448
00:27:00,586 --> 00:27:02,748
That's all very well
and good, Miss Clark,

449
00:27:02,921 --> 00:27:04,150
but it doesn't change the fact

450
00:27:04,323 --> 00:27:07,020
that you should've taken
yourself off the case.

451
00:27:07,192 --> 00:27:09,525
I tried. No one
knows that, but I did.

452
00:27:09,695 --> 00:27:11,755
And with all due
respect, Your Honor,

453
00:27:11,930 --> 00:27:15,492
all officers of the court have
personal lives and prejudices

454
00:27:15,667 --> 00:27:17,966
which they routinely set
aside in order to do their job.

455
00:27:18,137 --> 00:27:20,072
And I have done that.

456
00:27:20,372 --> 00:27:22,432
Again, contrary to
what the defendant says,

457
00:27:22,608 --> 00:27:24,042
I have not concocted evidence.

458
00:27:24,209 --> 00:27:25,268
Mr. Matlock is welcome

459
00:27:25,444 --> 00:27:27,242
to go over any and
all of my findings.

460
00:27:27,679 --> 00:27:30,205
In fact, if anything, the
delicacy of my situation

461
00:27:30,382 --> 00:27:33,375
has made me even more diligent
in the preparation of this case

462
00:27:33,552 --> 00:27:34,986
than I otherwise
might have been.

463
00:27:35,354 --> 00:27:36,788
If this case is appealed,

464
00:27:36,955 --> 00:27:39,390
it won't be because of
anything I have or haven't done.

465
00:27:39,558 --> 00:27:41,424
I promise you.

466
00:27:41,593 --> 00:27:44,791
There is no need
to declare a mistrial.

467
00:27:49,067 --> 00:27:51,627
Mr. Matlock, I haven't
heard your 2 cents.

468
00:27:52,905 --> 00:27:59,106
Oh, well, there's nothing to
be gained by starting over.

469
00:27:59,278 --> 00:28:02,806
Of course, I'm gonna take
a good look at everything.

470
00:28:02,981 --> 00:28:07,510
And if you mess with any evidence
or make another misstep in this case,

471
00:28:07,686 --> 00:28:09,951
I'll have a mistrial
in a heartbeat.

472
00:28:10,389 --> 00:28:11,550
Fine with me.

473
00:28:12,958 --> 00:28:16,292
In that case, court will
reconvene at 9 a.m. tomorrow.

474
00:28:19,565 --> 00:28:22,535
- What have you got to say?
- I'm sorry we have this whole mess.

475
00:28:22,701 --> 00:28:25,603
You should be. You
made me look like an idiot.

476
00:28:25,771 --> 00:28:27,239
Now, wait a minute.

477
00:28:27,406 --> 00:28:29,898
When I was first assigned this
case, I came in here to tell you

478
00:28:30,075 --> 00:28:31,839
that there might be
a conflict of interest.

479
00:28:32,010 --> 00:28:34,730
But before I could get a word out,
you told me how busy everyone was,

480
00:28:34,880 --> 00:28:37,320
how much trouble you had with
women trying to get out of work.

481
00:28:37,449 --> 00:28:39,509
How would I have looked
if I had to remove myself

482
00:28:39,685 --> 00:28:41,017
from the first case you gave me

483
00:28:41,186 --> 00:28:42,866
because I had an affair
with the defendant?

484
00:28:43,021 --> 00:28:44,990
You would've looked a
lot better than you do now.

485
00:28:47,793 --> 00:28:50,285
We're gonna have
to continue this later.

486
00:28:53,498 --> 00:28:56,525
Prosecuting a
former lover? Ha, ha!

487
00:28:56,702 --> 00:28:58,261
Bad form, Miss Clark.

488
00:28:59,071 --> 00:29:01,631
I'm only gonna say
this once, Mr. Stonehill.

489
00:29:01,807 --> 00:29:02,831
Back off.

490
00:29:03,008 --> 00:29:05,443
Or you'll do what,
stomp your little foot?

491
00:29:06,144 --> 00:29:08,670
Listen, I'm just tired of
seeing people come in here

492
00:29:08,847 --> 00:29:10,247
expecting special treatment

493
00:29:10,415 --> 00:29:12,748
just because they got kids,
bills and breasts. That's all.

494
00:29:14,119 --> 00:29:16,452
Miss Clark, Mr. Jenkins
wants to see you.

495
00:29:18,523 --> 00:29:21,652
The day I ask you or him
or anybody else around here

496
00:29:21,827 --> 00:29:24,490
for special treatment, don't
be surprised if you sprout wings,

497
00:29:24,663 --> 00:29:26,962
because that'll
be the day pigs fly.

498
00:29:33,538 --> 00:29:35,700
I just got off the phone
with Detective Remsen.

499
00:29:35,874 --> 00:29:37,754
Police have located
Rusty Stambler's girlfriend.

500
00:29:37,909 --> 00:29:39,707
Says she saw Jack
Gibson at Stambler's place

501
00:29:39,878 --> 00:29:41,710
around the time of the murder.

502
00:29:41,880 --> 00:29:44,042
Her name's Patricia
Price. Here's her address.

503
00:29:46,952 --> 00:29:48,784
All right, nothing like
what happened today

504
00:29:48,954 --> 00:29:51,185
is ever gonna happen again.

505
00:29:53,158 --> 00:29:55,423
I know I put you in a hard
place when this first began,

506
00:29:55,594 --> 00:29:59,759
but next time, speak up. I
wouldn't wanna have to fire you

507
00:29:59,931 --> 00:30:02,662
or see to it that you never
practice law in this state again.

508
00:30:23,121 --> 00:30:24,987
There we go.

509
00:30:25,157 --> 00:30:27,592
Okay, it ought to work now.

510
00:30:31,296 --> 00:30:33,197
- Thanks.
- Yep.

511
00:30:33,365 --> 00:30:36,699
Just don't ask me to
program your VCR, heh, okay?

512
00:30:43,141 --> 00:30:45,474
You and that woman DA, huh?

513
00:30:45,777 --> 00:30:47,177
Yep.

514
00:30:47,346 --> 00:30:48,546
Well, you got good taste, Dad.

515
00:30:48,714 --> 00:30:52,378
Terrible timing, but good taste.

516
00:30:54,252 --> 00:30:55,880
You know, Mike,

517
00:30:56,955 --> 00:30:58,995
I didn't tell anybody because
to be honest with you,

518
00:30:59,124 --> 00:31:00,649
I figured...

519
00:31:00,992 --> 00:31:03,120
Well, I just figured,
what could it hurt?

520
00:31:04,529 --> 00:31:06,896
I had no idea she was
gonna wind up coming after me

521
00:31:07,065 --> 00:31:09,000
tooth and nail like this,

522
00:31:09,167 --> 00:31:12,035
digging into my
business affairs,

523
00:31:13,672 --> 00:31:16,141
making me look like a crook.

524
00:31:21,380 --> 00:31:23,281
- Mike?
- Yeah?

525
00:31:23,448 --> 00:31:25,041
Turn around.

526
00:31:29,020 --> 00:31:30,716
Look at me.

527
00:31:33,959 --> 00:31:35,825
It was that one time,

528
00:31:35,994 --> 00:31:38,725
that one time
only, I swear to you.

529
00:31:38,964 --> 00:31:41,593
And I did not start
out to cheat anybody.

530
00:31:41,767 --> 00:31:43,167
It just happened.

531
00:31:43,335 --> 00:31:46,134
We were running behind,
there was a holdup on the rebar,

532
00:31:46,304 --> 00:31:48,344
so I used whatever scrap
was laying around the yard.

533
00:31:48,507 --> 00:31:49,531
That's okay.

534
00:31:49,708 --> 00:31:52,940
I mean, it's not like you were
endangering lives or anything.

535
00:31:54,179 --> 00:31:56,478
Come on, you gotta
stop worrying about it.

536
00:31:56,648 --> 00:31:59,516
Dad, you hired Ben
Matlock. He's the best, right?

537
00:32:00,051 --> 00:32:03,351
He's not gonna
let you down. Dad.

538
00:32:04,489 --> 00:32:05,889
Neither am I.

539
00:32:27,579 --> 00:32:29,445
- Mom?
- Yeah?

540
00:32:29,614 --> 00:32:32,083
I'm done painting
that chair downstairs.

541
00:32:32,250 --> 00:32:34,913
Good for you, honey. Why
don't you go get ready for bed?

542
00:32:35,086 --> 00:32:36,349
Mom?

543
00:32:36,521 --> 00:32:40,014
- Yeah?
- Molly says you're gonna kill me.

544
00:32:45,564 --> 00:32:47,897
She may have a point.

545
00:32:48,066 --> 00:32:49,967
He got it all
over the floor too.

546
00:32:50,135 --> 00:32:52,127
- Thank you, Molly.
- Mom?

547
00:32:52,304 --> 00:32:55,741
- Yes?
- Where'd Grandma die?

548
00:32:55,907 --> 00:32:57,808
In the hospital.

549
00:32:57,976 --> 00:33:00,138
He thinks this house is haunted.

550
00:33:00,312 --> 00:33:01,575
He does?

551
00:33:01,746 --> 00:33:02,941
Is it?

552
00:33:04,082 --> 00:33:05,448
Well, in a way, it is.

553
00:33:05,851 --> 00:33:06,910
Really?

554
00:33:07,385 --> 00:33:08,785
Like this.

555
00:33:08,954 --> 00:33:10,650
I grew up in this house.

556
00:33:10,822 --> 00:33:12,085
Every time I turn around,

557
00:33:12,257 --> 00:33:15,250
I think of something I
haven't thought of in 20 years.

558
00:33:15,427 --> 00:33:17,487
The way Grandpa
used to sit at his desk

559
00:33:17,662 --> 00:33:19,342
looking at pennies
with a magnifying glass,

560
00:33:19,498 --> 00:33:21,660
seeing what he was
gonna save for his collection.

561
00:33:21,833 --> 00:33:24,029
Or the way Grandma used
to sit at the kitchen table

562
00:33:24,202 --> 00:33:26,933
every Sunday night
painting her nails.

563
00:33:27,105 --> 00:33:29,768
It's like they're here.
Kind of like ghosts.

564
00:33:30,308 --> 00:33:33,039
- Ghosts?
- Happy ghosts.

565
00:33:33,211 --> 00:33:35,373
See? So stop
making me go with you

566
00:33:35,547 --> 00:33:37,072
every time you
need to go upstairs.

567
00:33:37,249 --> 00:33:38,877
- Okay?
- Okay.

568
00:33:39,618 --> 00:33:41,678
You been doing that,
Molly? Thanks. That's nice.

569
00:33:41,853 --> 00:33:44,379
Yeah. Well, you know how
bad he whines when he's scared.

570
00:33:46,758 --> 00:33:48,693
I love you two.

571
00:33:49,895 --> 00:33:52,330
Okay, close your eyes.

572
00:33:56,167 --> 00:33:58,033
Can I do that?

573
00:34:02,574 --> 00:34:05,567
And how would you describe your
relationship to Rusty, Miss Price?

574
00:34:05,744 --> 00:34:08,270
We were, you know, lovers.

575
00:34:08,446 --> 00:34:10,540
You were at his house
not only the night before,

576
00:34:10,715 --> 00:34:12,479
but the morning of his
murder. Is that right?

577
00:34:12,651 --> 00:34:13,846
Yeah, that's right.

578
00:34:14,853 --> 00:34:17,948
Miss Price, there's
something that's puzzling me.

579
00:34:18,323 --> 00:34:19,791
Why didn't you come forward

580
00:34:19,958 --> 00:34:22,086
when you first heard
Rusty was murdered?

581
00:34:22,260 --> 00:34:25,526
Two words: I'm married.

582
00:34:25,697 --> 00:34:28,895
Least I was until
all this hit the fan.

583
00:34:29,768 --> 00:34:31,566
Well, tell us what
you saw that morning.

584
00:34:31,736 --> 00:34:34,137
Well, see, my husband
was out of town,

585
00:34:34,306 --> 00:34:36,605
so I'd gone over to
Rusty's the night before

586
00:34:36,775 --> 00:34:39,108
to, you know, spend the night.

587
00:34:39,277 --> 00:34:40,836
But he wasn't feeling too good,

588
00:34:41,012 --> 00:34:43,208
so I left around midnight.

589
00:34:43,381 --> 00:34:45,141
Stopped by on my way
to work the next morning

590
00:34:45,283 --> 00:34:46,376
to see if he felt better.

591
00:34:46,785 --> 00:34:47,809
And what did you see?

592
00:34:47,986 --> 00:34:50,956
I parked my car a ways
down the street, like always.

593
00:34:51,122 --> 00:34:52,562
And as I was
getting out of the car,

594
00:34:52,724 --> 00:34:55,387
I saw this guy come around
the side of Rusty's house,

595
00:34:55,560 --> 00:34:58,359
from the back, and
go in the front door.

596
00:34:58,530 --> 00:35:00,328
Do you have any
idea what time it was?

597
00:35:00,498 --> 00:35:03,195
Seven-thirty. I know
because WFCY

598
00:35:03,368 --> 00:35:05,234
was playing its top-ten list.

599
00:35:05,403 --> 00:35:07,429
I didn't get out of the
car until it was over.

600
00:35:08,273 --> 00:35:10,469
Miss Price, I'd like you
to look around and tell me

601
00:35:10,642 --> 00:35:12,907
if the man you saw go into
Rusty's house that morning

602
00:35:13,078 --> 00:35:14,318
is here in the courtroom today.

603
00:35:15,780 --> 00:35:17,140
Yeah, that's him
right over there.

604
00:35:20,018 --> 00:35:21,850
Let the record show
that the witness pointed

605
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:23,886
to Jack Gibson, the defendant.

606
00:35:24,055 --> 00:35:25,921
- Nothing further.
- Ahem.

607
00:35:26,524 --> 00:35:33,055
Uh, when you say that
Rusty wasn't feeling well,

608
00:35:33,231 --> 00:35:35,427
how do you mean
he wasn't feeling well?

609
00:35:35,867 --> 00:35:37,836
Said he felt like
he was gonna hurl.

610
00:35:38,003 --> 00:35:39,027
Ooh.

611
00:35:39,204 --> 00:35:41,935
Was that before or
after you had some wine?

612
00:35:42,107 --> 00:35:43,632
Both.

613
00:35:43,808 --> 00:35:46,403
Rusty thought drinking
something might help.

614
00:35:46,578 --> 00:35:49,446
It didn't, so I split.

615
00:35:49,614 --> 00:35:51,549
If he had something,
I didn't wanna catch it.

616
00:35:51,716 --> 00:35:53,810
- Did you leave in a hurry?
- No.

617
00:35:53,985 --> 00:35:56,921
Well, how come you left
your makeup case behind?

618
00:35:57,889 --> 00:35:59,084
I forgot it.

619
00:35:59,491 --> 00:36:01,221
You left your makeup case behind

620
00:36:01,393 --> 00:36:05,592
because you went stomping out of
Rusty's house in a huff, didn't you?

621
00:36:05,764 --> 00:36:07,323
- What do you mean?
- Well, I guess

622
00:36:07,499 --> 00:36:10,059
because after a couple
of glasses of wine,

623
00:36:10,235 --> 00:36:11,635
he started making threats

624
00:36:11,803 --> 00:36:13,931
about telling your
husband about your affair.

625
00:36:14,105 --> 00:36:16,199
- No.
- Only you didn't like that.

626
00:36:16,374 --> 00:36:17,967
Made you mad and you left.

627
00:36:18,143 --> 00:36:20,023
But the next morning, you
came back to make sure

628
00:36:20,178 --> 00:36:21,858
he didn't make any
threats like that again.

629
00:36:22,013 --> 00:36:23,447
Now, you just wait a minute.

630
00:36:23,615 --> 00:36:26,449
I went over there that morning
to see how he was doing,

631
00:36:26,618 --> 00:36:28,018
only I never got the chance

632
00:36:28,186 --> 00:36:30,678
because that man right
there went in ahead of me.

633
00:36:30,855 --> 00:36:33,916
He was wearing blue jeans
and a dark-colored jacket,

634
00:36:34,092 --> 00:36:35,151
and he was in such a hurry

635
00:36:35,326 --> 00:36:37,227
he put his foot right
through one of the steps.

636
00:36:37,395 --> 00:36:39,057
And that's the truth, mister.

637
00:36:39,230 --> 00:36:41,597
You saw all that while
you were parking your car,

638
00:36:41,766 --> 00:36:44,326
listening to the radio?

639
00:36:51,876 --> 00:36:53,037
Mr. Matlock?

640
00:36:53,645 --> 00:36:55,011
Uh...

641
00:36:55,814 --> 00:37:00,013
Defense requests
a 15-minute recess.

642
00:37:02,353 --> 00:37:05,118
- Ben, what's going on?
- You did it.

643
00:37:05,290 --> 00:37:07,282
You didn't tell me of
your affair with Katie

644
00:37:07,459 --> 00:37:10,190
because you was hoping she'd
give you some kind of a break.

645
00:37:10,361 --> 00:37:12,592
- Ben, don't be ridic...
- You stopped me from stepping

646
00:37:12,764 --> 00:37:15,165
on that step because
you knew it was broken

647
00:37:15,333 --> 00:37:17,029
because you were
the one who broke it.

648
00:37:17,202 --> 00:37:19,933
- Ben, you are wrong.
- I don't wanna hear it.

649
00:37:23,408 --> 00:37:25,240
All right, Ben, hear this.

650
00:37:25,410 --> 00:37:27,379
I'm entitled to the
best possible defense,

651
00:37:27,545 --> 00:37:29,946
and you are ethically
bound to give me that.

652
00:37:30,115 --> 00:37:31,549
Besides, I paid
you a lot of money,

653
00:37:31,716 --> 00:37:34,584
so why don't you just
get out there and earn it?

654
00:37:47,232 --> 00:37:49,360
- Right here.
- Oh, thanks.

655
00:37:49,934 --> 00:37:51,903
Well, thanks for coming.

656
00:37:52,070 --> 00:37:55,199
- As you see, I'm still getting settled.
- Yeah.

657
00:37:55,373 --> 00:37:57,467
Oh, here, have a seat.

658
00:37:57,642 --> 00:38:00,009
- Where are you gonna sit?
- Oh, right here's fine.

659
00:38:00,178 --> 00:38:01,840
Oh, okay.

660
00:38:06,117 --> 00:38:09,884
This case has come a long way
since we first talked, Mr. Matlock.

661
00:38:10,054 --> 00:38:11,078
I've established motive

662
00:38:11,256 --> 00:38:13,816
and placed your client
at the scene of the crime.

663
00:38:13,992 --> 00:38:15,585
In other words, I've got him.

664
00:38:16,694 --> 00:38:20,529
So in order to save us
all a lot of time and money,

665
00:38:20,698 --> 00:38:22,894
I'm prepared to offer
your client a deal.

666
00:38:25,069 --> 00:38:26,128
What kind of a deal?

667
00:38:26,905 --> 00:38:30,034
He pleads guilty, I reduce the
charge to second degree murder.

668
00:38:30,208 --> 00:38:32,837
He does a maximum
10 years in prison.

669
00:38:36,614 --> 00:38:38,378
He won't go for it.

670
00:38:38,550 --> 00:38:41,782
Of course, you probably
know that better than I do.

671
00:38:42,854 --> 00:38:44,948
Well, you tell him anyway.

672
00:38:45,123 --> 00:38:47,820
And make sure he understands
this is a one-time deal.

673
00:38:47,992 --> 00:38:49,187
He turns it down, that's it.

674
00:38:49,360 --> 00:38:51,090
He'll be at the
mercy of the court.

675
00:38:52,463 --> 00:38:54,523
Okay, I'll tell him.

676
00:38:55,800 --> 00:38:58,326
I wouldn't get my
hopes up, though.

677
00:39:00,104 --> 00:39:01,333
Mr. Matlock,

678
00:39:02,674 --> 00:39:04,370
these are not my hopes.

679
00:39:04,843 --> 00:39:08,177
This was District Attorney
Jenkins' idea, believe me.

680
00:39:08,346 --> 00:39:10,815
I did my best to
talk him out of it.

681
00:39:10,982 --> 00:39:13,110
I think I could
get a conviction.

682
00:39:14,786 --> 00:39:16,220
But he doesn't.

683
00:39:18,256 --> 00:39:20,487
He's just trying
to be efficient.

684
00:39:22,760 --> 00:39:24,524
Efficient.

685
00:39:36,541 --> 00:39:40,774
Now, this is a picture of
Rusty Stambler's street

686
00:39:40,945 --> 00:39:43,312
taken from outside a car parked

687
00:39:43,481 --> 00:39:47,179
where approximately you said
your car was parked, about here.

688
00:39:47,352 --> 00:39:50,186
Now, can you pick out
Rusty's house from this picture?

689
00:39:50,355 --> 00:39:52,620
- Well, yeah, right there.
- Uh-huh.

690
00:39:52,790 --> 00:39:56,090
And this is the view of the
house that you saw that morning?

691
00:39:56,561 --> 00:39:57,790
Yeah.

692
00:39:57,962 --> 00:40:00,158
There's a lot of
bushes along here,

693
00:40:00,331 --> 00:40:02,061
and you say you saw a man

694
00:40:02,233 --> 00:40:04,964
walking along the
side of the house.

695
00:40:05,136 --> 00:40:07,696
You'd have seen him in
profile and through the bushes,

696
00:40:07,872 --> 00:40:09,306
- wouldn't you?
- Yeah.

697
00:40:09,474 --> 00:40:12,672
And you'd see his back
as he went up the steps

698
00:40:12,844 --> 00:40:14,870
and inside the
house, is that right?

699
00:40:15,046 --> 00:40:16,639
- I guess.
- Let's see.

700
00:40:16,814 --> 00:40:18,715
How far would you say
you were parked away?

701
00:40:18,883 --> 00:40:21,580
About, what, 50
yards, would you say?

702
00:40:21,753 --> 00:40:23,722
- I don't know.
- Well, with the bushes

703
00:40:23,888 --> 00:40:26,824
and the profile and
his back and all,

704
00:40:26,991 --> 00:40:30,792
you weren't exactly up
close and personal, were you?

705
00:40:30,962 --> 00:40:32,726
I know what I saw.

706
00:40:32,897 --> 00:40:35,196
Thank you. No more questions.

707
00:40:35,366 --> 00:40:37,767
Defense calls Mr. Michael
Gibson to the stand.

708
00:40:54,218 --> 00:40:57,086
Did you know the
decedent, Rusty Stambler?

709
00:40:57,956 --> 00:40:59,948
I'd met him a couple of
times through my father,

710
00:41:00,124 --> 00:41:01,319
but I didn't really know him.

711
00:41:01,492 --> 00:41:03,461
- So you weren't friends?
- No.

712
00:41:03,628 --> 00:41:05,460
You have any
contact with him at all?

713
00:41:05,630 --> 00:41:07,121
No.

714
00:41:07,298 --> 00:41:08,596
Hm.

715
00:41:08,800 --> 00:41:14,000
Well, why is it that
according to the records

716
00:41:14,172 --> 00:41:18,132
I subpoenaed from
the phone company,

717
00:41:18,443 --> 00:41:22,244
one call was made from
his house to your apartment,

718
00:41:22,413 --> 00:41:26,145
and five calls were made
from your place to his place

719
00:41:26,317 --> 00:41:29,412
in the two weeks
prior to the murder?

720
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:41,097
Rusty Stambler told me my
father was defrauding clients

721
00:41:42,033 --> 00:41:44,593
by substituting materials
he had specified in his bids

722
00:41:44,769 --> 00:41:45,998
for cheaper ones.

723
00:41:46,170 --> 00:41:49,299
He told me he was gonna report
him, so I tried to talk him out of it.

724
00:41:49,941 --> 00:41:51,375
Does your father know this?

725
00:41:53,277 --> 00:41:55,576
No, I never told him.

726
00:41:55,747 --> 00:41:57,978
I knew how much it
would embarrass him.

727
00:42:02,153 --> 00:42:04,145
You love your father
very much, don't you?

728
00:42:05,123 --> 00:42:06,523
Yeah.

729
00:42:09,494 --> 00:42:12,658
- How tall are you?
- Five-eleven.

730
00:42:13,464 --> 00:42:14,989
How tall is your father?

731
00:42:16,334 --> 00:42:17,393
About the same.

732
00:42:18,703 --> 00:42:21,832
Same height, same color hair.

733
00:42:22,006 --> 00:42:23,634
People ever confuse you?

734
00:42:24,876 --> 00:42:27,539
- It happens.
- Yeah, yeah.

735
00:42:29,247 --> 00:42:30,977
Do you own a pair of jeans?

736
00:42:32,350 --> 00:42:33,750
Of course. Who doesn't?

737
00:42:33,918 --> 00:42:35,682
Okay.

738
00:42:35,853 --> 00:42:38,823
Where were you between 7 and 8

739
00:42:38,990 --> 00:42:40,891
- the morning of the murder?
- At home.

740
00:42:41,392 --> 00:42:42,826
- All alone?
- Yes.

741
00:42:46,831 --> 00:42:49,892
I subpoenaed your bank records.

742
00:42:51,102 --> 00:42:56,871
You withdrew $75,000 in cash

743
00:42:57,041 --> 00:42:59,601
from your market-rate
savings account

744
00:42:59,777 --> 00:43:01,040
two days before the murder.

745
00:43:01,212 --> 00:43:02,305
- Remember that?
- Yeah.

746
00:43:02,480 --> 00:43:03,948
And what's interesting

747
00:43:04,115 --> 00:43:07,017
is before the bank
closed that same day,

748
00:43:07,185 --> 00:43:08,915
you redeposited that money.

749
00:43:09,087 --> 00:43:10,180
Now, why'd you do that?

750
00:43:11,489 --> 00:43:14,391
I was gonna loan
the money to a friend.

751
00:43:14,559 --> 00:43:17,222
- He decided he didn't need it after all.
- What friend?

752
00:43:19,263 --> 00:43:20,458
Who was the money for?

753
00:43:23,401 --> 00:43:25,063
Your Honor,

754
00:43:25,636 --> 00:43:29,937
I ask that you instruct this witness
to give me the names of the person

755
00:43:30,108 --> 00:43:32,168
or persons this money was for

756
00:43:32,343 --> 00:43:33,538
and a recess from court

757
00:43:33,711 --> 00:43:36,146
so I can check out
the details immediately.

758
00:43:37,081 --> 00:43:38,777
The witness will
answer the question.

759
00:43:48,693 --> 00:43:50,628
It wasn't a loan, was it?

760
00:43:51,562 --> 00:43:53,155
It was hush money, wasn't it?

761
00:43:53,331 --> 00:43:55,061
Only Rusty wouldn't take it.

762
00:43:55,233 --> 00:43:57,668
So to protect the
father you adore

763
00:43:57,835 --> 00:44:01,670
from public humiliation
and financial ruin,

764
00:44:01,839 --> 00:44:05,139
you went over to Rusty's house
to shut him up for good, didn't you?

765
00:44:05,309 --> 00:44:07,540
- No!
- It was you Pat Price saw

766
00:44:07,712 --> 00:44:09,442
outside his house that day.

767
00:44:09,614 --> 00:44:12,277
It was you who went in there and
strangled him to death, wasn't it?

768
00:44:12,450 --> 00:44:13,975
No!

769
00:44:18,222 --> 00:44:19,986
It wasn't him.

770
00:44:27,165 --> 00:44:28,861
It was me.

771
00:44:40,211 --> 00:44:42,976
Ew, cool. Just
about to kill him.

772
00:44:43,281 --> 00:44:46,115
Ew, neat. All his guts
came out on my sword.

773
00:44:46,284 --> 00:44:48,685
Wait till you see what
happens when you kill this guy.

774
00:44:49,253 --> 00:44:50,551
Yes.

775
00:44:52,924 --> 00:44:54,222
Miss Clark?

776
00:44:54,392 --> 00:44:57,851
Mr. Matlock, come in.
Turn those things off, please.

777
00:44:58,029 --> 00:44:59,998
But, Mom, I'm just
about to kill this guy.

778
00:45:00,164 --> 00:45:01,928
Yeah, it's really cool
when you kill him,

779
00:45:02,099 --> 00:45:04,659
- because his brains come out...
- Turn it off.

780
00:45:05,436 --> 00:45:08,838
- Jeez, Mom.
- I just came by to give congratulations.

781
00:45:09,240 --> 00:45:12,074
Oh, please. You're the one who
won that case and you know it.

782
00:45:12,243 --> 00:45:13,836
No, you're the one
who discovered Jack

783
00:45:14,011 --> 00:45:15,211
was switching those materials.

784
00:45:15,379 --> 00:45:17,177
I might never have
picked up on that.

785
00:45:17,348 --> 00:45:19,044
Oh, I think you would've.

786
00:45:19,217 --> 00:45:20,412
But thanks, anyway.

787
00:45:20,585 --> 00:45:23,749
At this stage in my career, I need
all the encouragement I can get.

788
00:45:23,921 --> 00:45:26,852
Mom says next time,
she's gonna whip your...

789
00:45:27,024 --> 00:45:29,016
I never did.

